INT. MUSEUM OF CLOCKS - VAULT CORRIDOR - NIGHT

A hush of ticking. Glass cases of pocket watches line the walls. At the end, a steel vault door the color of old pewter.

A MAN in a night guard's uniform kneels at the door with a stethoscope pressed against the lock. A WOMAN in a cleaner's smock stands behind him with a mop bucket she has not moved in several minutes.

MAN
Quiet.

WOMAN
I am being quiet.

MAN
You're breathing.

WOMAN
I'm told it's mandatory.

He turns the dial a hair. Tick. Tick. The stethoscope clicks. He sits back.

MAN (CONT'D)
That's three. One more.

WOMAN
You said that two numbers ago.

MAN
I was optimistic.

Footsteps echo far away. Both freeze. The footsteps stop. A radio crackles in the distance and goes silent.

WOMAN
(whispering)
How long?

MAN
Ninety seconds.

WOMAN
Till what?

MAN
Till he comes round again.

She lifts the mop from the bucket like a spear. He shakes his head and takes it from her, sets it against the wall, and presses the stethoscope back to the lock.

The dial turns. A deep, final clunk. The door sighs open an inch.

MAN (CONT'D)
Got it.

WOMAN
Of course you did.

Inside, on a velvet plinth, a single gold clock the size of a fist ticks serenely.

MAN
Sixteen-forty-two. Made for a king who never lived to wind it.

WOMAN
Oh, I like him already.

She steps forward. A red laser blinks across the doorway at ankle height. They both see it at the same moment.

MAN
I don't remember that.

WOMAN ^
I don't remember that.

MAN
(to himself)
Of course. Of course there's a laser.

The woman crouches, studies it, and pulls a compact mirror from her pocket. She holds it at an angle. The beam bends and strikes the wall.

WOMAN
There. Satisfied?

MAN
Reasonably.

She steps through. The man follows, stepping high.

At the plinth, she lifts the gold clock. It stops ticking.

Silence. They look at each other.

MAN (CONT'D)
That's not good.

She leans in and reads the name stitched on his guard's jacket.

WOMAN
Marcus?

The man closes his eyes.

MARCUS
(whisper)
Don't start.

WOMAN
You told me your name was Gil.

MARCUS
It's a long night.

Footsteps again, closer now. Marcus takes the clock from her hands, sets it back on the plinth, and the ticking resumes. They look at it, then at each other. Marcus smiles, nervous and delighted.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
Run.
